requirements:
Build a precise sparse-matrix library. Every numbered rule is normative.

R1. Implement in Rust as a SINGLE source file that compiles with `rustc --edition 2024`, std only. Output ONLY the Rust source code inside ```rust fences. Do not use inner attributes or inner documentation comments.

R2. Implement exactly the public API below. Additional private helpers are allowed. Struct storage is private except Entry fields. All named API items are pub. Axis has Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq; Entry has Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq; Sparse, Csr (when present), and Error have Clone, Debug, PartialEq. Equality compares shape and canonical contents. There is no main function requirement.

R3. MAX_DIM is 4096. Each constructor checks dimensions first: rows > MAX_DIM gives Shape{axis:Row,size:rows}, otherwise cols > MAX_DIM gives Shape{axis:Column,size:cols}. Boundary 4096 is allowed. These checks precede every other check.

R4. Shapes may have zero rows and/or columns. Empty COO input succeeds for any allowed shape. Entries in a zero-sized axis are out of bounds. No dense allocation is required.

R5. from_coo first validates entries in original slice order. For each entry, check row >= rows (Bounds with axis Row), then col >= cols (axis Column), then value finiteness. Bounds includes the original zero-based entry index, offending index and axis limit. Stop at the first failing entry/check.

R6. Every NaN, positive infinity, or negative infinity in COO gives NonFinite{entry}; positive and negative finite values, both signed zeros, and subnormals are allowed. Bounds at that entry take priority over NonFinite.

R7. Complete the whole COO validation pass before performing any duplicate summation. Thus an invalid later entry takes precedence over an earlier possible summation overflow.

R8. After validation, process entries in original slice order. For each coordinate start its sum at +0.0 and perform one ordinary f64 addition per occurrence, including zeros. Do not sort values, reassociate, compensate, or sum separately by sign.

R9. If any such addition produces a nonfinite result, immediately return SumOverflow{entry} using the original index of the entry being added. A later cancelling entry does not rescue overflow.

R10. Only after all sums succeed, omit every coordinate whose final sum compares equal to 0.0, including negative zero. A coordinate that temporarily becomes zero must still be allowed later contributions.

R11. Keep every finite nonzero final sum, including the smallest subnormal; apply no epsilon or rounding-to-zero policy.

R12. Canonical entries contain exactly one Entry per retained coordinate, sorted by ascending row and then ascending column, irrespective of input ordering.

R13. Sparse::shape returns the supplied (rows,cols) even for empty/cancelled matrices. Sparse::entries returns a borrowed slice of the canonical entries with their exact f64 values.

R14. from_coo must not change the caller's slice. Cloning a matrix preserves shape and contents; accessor calls do not mutate it.

R15. All specified operations are deterministic and return Result errors rather than panic on malformed inputs. For allowed dimensions and ordinary test-sized slices, do not impose extra size limits.

R16. All entry, slot, row, column and vector indices in errors are zero-based; text line numbers (when present) are one-based. Do not renumber COO entries after sorting or coalescing.

R17. Use ordinary IEEE-754 f64 operations in the specified order, with no fused multiply-add, approximate comparisons, or arbitrary-precision replacement. Successful public numeric results contain only finite values.

R18. Error checking order is part of the contract. Return only the first error according to the explicitly specified passes, not an aggregate or a more severe later error.

R19. Sparse::to_csr produces canonical compressed sparse rows: row_ptr has rows+1 elements, starts at 0, and row_ptr[r+1] equals the count of entries in rows <= r. Empty rows repeat the preceding pointer. col_indices and values follow canonical COO order.

R20. Csr accessors borrow their arrays; shape returns (rows,cols). to_coo returns the equivalent canonical Sparse, preserving shape and values. Conversions do not mutate inputs.

R21. Csr::from_parts validates shape as above, then row_ptr.len()==rows+1 (RowPtrLength), then col_idx.len()==values.len() (ColumnValueLength). Error fields expected/actual and columns/values are the respective lengths.

R22. Next from_parts checks row_ptr[0]==0 (RowPtrStart{found}), then row_ptr[rows]==values.len() (RowPtrEnd{found,expected}). These endpoint checks precede monotonicity and all slot checks, including for rows=0.

R23. Next scan r=0..rows for the first row_ptr[r]>row_ptr[r+1], returning RowPtrOrder{row:r}. Do this before dereferencing any slots; huge invalid interior pointers must yield errors without panicking.

R24. Finally scan rows ascending and slots ascending within rows. At slot s, check col_idx[s]<cols (ColumnBounds{slot,column,limit}), then strict increase relative to the previous column of this same row (ColumnOrder{slot}), then finite value (NonFiniteValue{slot}), then value != 0.0 (ExplicitZero{slot}). No previous-column comparison crosses a row boundary. from_parts accepts only canonical arrays; it never sorts, merges or removes zeros.

R25. mul_vector first checks x.len()==cols (VectorLength{expected:cols,actual:x.len()}), then scans all x indices ascending for nonfinite values (NonFiniteVector{index}), even if a column is unused or the matrix has no rows. Complete validation before arithmetic.

R26. mul_vector scans rows ascending, then that row's stored slots ascending. Compute p=value*x[column] separately; nonfinite p returns ProductOverflow{row,column} before adding it to the row accumulator.

R27. Start each row accumulator at +0.0, add each p once in slot order, and after each addition return AccumulationOverflow{row,column} if nonfinite. Underflow to zero is allowed. Do not skip arithmetic when x[column] is zero.

R28. mul_vector returns exactly rows values in row order, including zero for empty rows. Normalize any final zero to +0.0. Do not mutate the matrix or x.

Exact public API (declarations below describe signatures, not compilable method bodies):
```rust
pub const MAX_DIM: usize = 4096;
pub enum Axis { Row, Column }
pub struct Entry { pub row: usize, pub col: usize, pub value: f64 }
pub struct Sparse { /* private */ }
impl Sparse {
    pub fn from_coo(rows: usize, cols: usize, entries: &[Entry]) -> Result<Self, Error>;
    pub fn shape(&self) -> (usize, usize);
    pub fn entries(&self) -> &[Entry];
}
pub struct Csr { /* private */ }
impl Sparse { pub fn to_csr(&self) -> Csr; }
impl Csr {
    pub fn from_parts(rows: usize, cols: usize, row_ptr: Vec<usize>, col_idx: Vec<usize>, values: Vec<f64>) -> Result<Self, Error>;
    pub fn shape(&self) -> (usize, usize);
    pub fn row_ptr(&self) -> &[usize];
    pub fn col_indices(&self) -> &[usize];
    pub fn values(&self) -> &[f64];
    pub fn to_coo(&self) -> Sparse;
    pub fn mul_vector(&self, x: &[f64]) -> Result<Vec<f64>, Error>;
}
pub enum Error {
    Shape { axis: Axis, size: usize },
    Bounds { entry: usize, axis: Axis, index: usize, limit: usize },
    NonFinite { entry: usize },
    SumOverflow { entry: usize },

    RowPtrLength { expected: usize, actual: usize },
    ColumnValueLength { columns: usize, values: usize },
    RowPtrStart { found: usize },
    RowPtrEnd { found: usize, expected: usize },
    RowPtrOrder { row: usize },
    ColumnBounds { slot: usize, column: usize, limit: usize },
    ColumnOrder { slot: usize },
    NonFiniteValue { slot: usize },
    ExplicitZero { slot: usize },
    VectorLength { expected: usize, actual: usize },
    NonFiniteVector { index: usize },
    ProductOverflow { row: usize, column: usize },
    AccumulationOverflow { row: usize, column: usize },
}
```
